 ## Overview
-Macrocell Grid Traversal is a hierarchical acceleration technique for ray tracing uniform voxel grids, designed to skip empty regions of the volume by superimposing a coarser grid over the fine data structure [S1, S2]. It differs from flat grid traversal by introducing a two-level hierarchy where the coarse level acts as a boolean occupancy map for blocks of fine cells, allowing the ray to bypass entire $M \times M \times M$ sections of the volume in a single step [S1, S2]. This method is specifically optimized for animated scenes with medium-sized grids where the acceleration structure is rebuilt every frame, prioritizing low build overhead over complex hierarchical balancing [S1, S2].
+Macrocell Grid Traversal is a hierarchical acceleration technique for ray tracing uniform voxel grids, designed to skip empty regions of the volume by superimposing a coarser grid over the fine data structure [S1, S2]. It differs from flat grid traversal by introducing a two-level hierarchy where the coarse level acts as a boolean occupancy map for blocks of fine cells, allowing the ray to bypass entire $M \times M \times M$ sections of the volume in a single step [S1, S2]. The method superimposes a coarse grid over the fine grid, using boolean flags to indicate occupancy of $M \times M \times M$ blocks [S1, S2]. It was designed to address the performance limitations of flat grid traversal in animated scenes, where the acceleration structure must be rebuilt every frame [S1, S2]. This approach was chosen for its simplicity and low build overhead, making it viable for medium-sized animated scenes [S1, S2].
 
